.TH CTPOOL_FUTURE_CREATE_DETACHED 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_future_create_detached \- create a standalone future with no pool or queued task
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool_future *ctpool_future_create_detached(char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates a future object that no cthread_pool task queue backs. An
outside producer must call ctpool_future_fulfill exactly one time to
deliver the result. An engine driven by events, which is not itself a
ctpool worker, is one such producer.

The future that comes back has a reference count of 2 on success. That
mirrors the convention of ctpool_submit_future. The caller holds one
reference, and ctpool_future_free releases it. The producer holds the
other, and ctpool_future_fulfill releases it. Each ctpool_future_get
call that runs on the future adds one more.

The library frees the struct after every reference goes, in whatever
order they go. A call to ctpool_future_free can come before a call to
ctpool_future_fulfill. Either call can come while other threads still
wait in ctpool_future_get. This is exactly how a future that a pool
backs behaves.

This function always uses the standard malloc and free. It does not use
any custom allocator. The futures of ctpool_submit_future work the same
way.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
err_str   Optional pointer to receive an error string on failure
          (pass NULL to ignore)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool_future handle (not yet done) on success.
Returns NULL on allocation failure.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_future_fulfill (3),
.BR ctpool_future_get (3),
.BR ctpool_future_done (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
